  {"type": "split", "h2": "Repair or replace: the rule we use for Ottawa furnaces", "photo": "sys",
   "html": "<p>Every furnace repair in Ottawa comes with a question our technician has to answer honestly: will this furnace make it through the rest of this winter, and the next one?</p><p>Our rule has three parts. First, the age. A furnace under ten years old with a failed ignitor, flame sensor, capacitor or pressure switch gets repaired, no debate. Second, the part. An inducer motor, a variable-speed blower or a control board on a furnace past fifteen years is the first domino, and the next part usually isn't far behind. Third, the heat exchanger. If it's cracked, the furnace is unsafe and the conversation is over.</p><p>Ottawa adds a fourth. A furnace that failed once in a cold snap has shown you where it's weak. If replacement is the right call, a Lennox or Midea two-stage, variable-speed model at 96% or 97% AFUE runs longer, quieter cycles and burns less gas. Add a cold-climate heat pump as a hybrid later, or at the same time, and the furnace becomes the backup instead of the workhorse. Our <a href='/ottawa/'>Ottawa HVAC page</a> walks through that hybrid setup.</p>"},
